I haven't heard of Llewellyn in this context, but Madoc ap Gwenedd was
supposed to have sailed west in 1120 or so, and made his way from the
Gulf of Mexico to the Tennessee Valley; then remnants of his people
are said to have moved further North and settled with the Mandan
Indians.

It's a very widespread traveller's tale. I ran across it first in an
early history of Tennessee, where some stone fortifications on a bluff
above the Tennessee were supposedly left behind by some white settlers
from long before.

There is a pretty good if romance-ish novelization of it in two books
by <memory lapse> titled _Madoc_ and _Madoc's Hundred_. I didn't bother
getting the second one.

The famous painter Caitlin (sp? No, not Chaitin!) is supposed to have
painted blue-eyed Mandans, but I have not been able to find such a
picture in books of his works (maybe they were grue?).

Also, not nearly all Welshmen have blond hair.

Traveller's tales are fun. I believe passionately in them between 2 and
4 am daily, and most Saturday mornings. There was also St. Brendan from
Eire, similar type of tale.

If you want a flood of quirky evidence of early European visitation,
check out _America B.C._ by Barry Fell.

