Ooh, this seems to be a good time to open another window
and do some comments:

>Wolves sho are familiar with humans tend to be very circumspect about
>them -- likely of survival value for both species...

Having seen a "wolf" (more about the quotes later) in the wild I think
that "circumspect" describes their behavior in a very concise and
accurate way.

>...(Actually, some biologists will tell you that they are the
>same species...)

Well, the wolf I saw, (forgot the latin name for the species, which
you will see is just as well), the Red Wolf native to Louisiana and
the Gulf Coast, was once thought to be going extinct and being replaced
by wolf/coyote/dog hybrids or pure coyotes, _but_ someone found some
Red Wolf pelts taken during the 18th century and found out they were
hybrid coyote/wolf... which IMHO would tend to make coyotes and
wolves subspecies of each other, with intermediate forms...

The main difference would seem to be in the memetic programming.
(I'm not sure if that deserves a smiley).

Also: I haven't seen JP yet, but from what I've heard, large brown
bears would probably be comparable. They're faster than horses, can't
be killed with anything less powerful than a 50 caliber Browning
(and I *doubt* a 50 caliber pistol round would suffice), and have
a brain the size of a gorillas (or larger; it's a trait they share
with Polar Bears). While not actively social, they are semi-social, and
I don't know what would happen to a group of velociraptors that attacked
a brown bear when others were within sight. (Or out of sight; for a
predator that weighs on the order of a metric ton and runs faster than
a horse, they have an unexpected reputation of being able to attack from
behind, and _not_ because the person is running away).

Finally, is anyone sure velociraptors would be totally safe attacking
suitably trained horses?

